  "icon": "switch",
  "color": "#dc2626",
  "sourceUrl": "https://e.huawei.com/marketingcloud/pep/asset/20000001/Material/0f9e00e3f2ca431693884a01667d7ec6/M3T1A669N1188254857740624027/OptiXtrans%20E9600%20Family%20-.pdf",
  "notes": "Stated card complement, not a fixed chassis figure: the E9624 brochure prints 24 small service slots in 1:1 cross-connect mode (12 large slots; one 11 U slot splits into two 5.5 U slots), so \"24x TNG1N210\" is what 240 line ports means. A partly filled shelf, the same shelf in 1:3 mode (20 small slots - IU6, IU7, IU18 and IU19 unusable), or one carrying TNG1N206 (6x 10G), TNG1N401 (1x 100G), the 20x10G TNV3N220/TNV5N220, or the OTN tributary and packet boards the E9624 column also admits (TNG1T206, TNG1T212, TNV1T410, TNV2E224) is a different box - model it separately rather than reusing this one. Same convention as devices/huawei/optixtrans-e9612.json, optixtrans-e9605.json and optixtrans-e6616.json.\n\nFrame, from the vendor brochure cited as sourceUrl (OptiXtrans E9600 Family, 4 pp, 277,705 B, read 2026-11-18, specification table, E9624 column): 747.2 (H) x 442 (W) x 295 (D) mm; \"Max. number of service board slots - 1:1 cross-connect mode: 12 large slots or 24 small slots; 1:3 cross-connect mode: 10 large slots or 20 small slots\", with the NOTE \"The E9624 subrack supports slot splitting. One 11 U slot of the E9624 subrack can be split into two 5.5 U slots.\" Electrical switching 4.8 Tbit/s OSUflex/ODUk, 4.8 Tbit/s packet services, 1.92 Tbit/s VC-4, 160 Gbit/s VC-3/VC-12, 1-20 degree ROADM.\n\nSlot numbering and the 1:3 caveat are vendor-sourced: TNG2DAP chapter, section 1.9 Valid Slots, Table 1 - the E9624 row reads 1 occupied slot, physical and logical IU1 to IU24, with the footnote \"In the 1:3 mode of an E9624 subrack, service boards cannot be installed in slots IU6, IU7, IU18, and IU19\" (https://e-file.huawei.com/marketingcloud/pep/asset/20000001/Material/e75c775ac71144e6b522d7cef1601604/M3T1A669N1188254864652623965/TNG2DAP%20-.pdf, HTTP 200, application/pdf, 871,676 B, read 2026-11-18). Twenty-four minus those four is the 20 small slots the brochure prints for 1:3 mode.\n\nBoard validity and port count: the OptiXtrans E9600 (E9624/E9612/E9605) Boards Overview chapter, Table 3 - the TNG1N206/TNG1N210 row prints \"1:1 mode: Y / 1:3 mode: Y\" under the E9624 column - and Table 1, which names the board \"TNG1N210: 10 x 10 Gbit/s Line Service Processing Board\". That chapter is EDOC1100126589BEJ09047 and its vendor copy (OptiXtrans E9600 Board Datasheet, EDOC1100163901) answers 302 -> /view/notFound for every user-agent tried, so the text was read from the verbatim partner copy at https://www.actfornet.com/solutions/solution/OptiXtrans_E9600_Boards_Overview (HTTP 200, 226,637 B, read 2026-11-18), whose 127 support.huawei.com/hedex topic links identify the vendor chapter it reproduces. The same table shows why this shelf is broader than its siblings: OTN tributary rows (TNG1T206, TNG1T212, TNV1T410) and packet rows (TNV2E224) read Y under the E9624 column and NA under E9612/E9605.\n\nThe 24 is the service range, and it excludes the cross-connect boards. Huawei Common Criteria Security Target EUCC-3089-2026-004 (https://certification.enisa.europa.eu/document/download/ab9c6a55-bf65-4095-99bb-4e947a28156c_en?filename=EUCC-3089-2026-004%20Security%20Target.pdf, HTTP 200, 3,009,787 B, issue 0.19 dated 2026-10-14, read 2026-11-18) puts \"OptiX OSN 9800 M24 / OptiXtrans E9624\" on one row of Table 1-1 and places the SCC board in \"CXP: IU71, IU72\", outside IU1-IU24. That row is also what joins the carrier name to the enterprise one, which is why the TNG1N210 chapter valid-slot line for \"OSN 9800 M24\" - 1:1 IU1-IU24, 1:3 IU1-IU5, IU8-IU17, IU20-IU24, single-slot board 237.1 x 30.5 x 220 mm, 64 W typical / 67 W maximum (reprint https://www.thunder-link.com/tng1n210.html) - describes this subrack. The cross-connect boards themselves are not modelled: no client ports, and the engine has no cross-connect concept, so capabilities stays cli.\n\nLine side is OTU2/OTU2e over DWDM, not Ethernet. The engine has no OTN type, so the ports are filed as ethernet/sfp+ - the physical cage - and the protocol claim is written down here instead. The link validator will therefore accept a 10GBASE-LR peer on a port that only carries OTU2: that is the house convention, shared by devices/huawei/optixtrans-e9612.json, optixtrans-e9605.json, optixtrans-e6616.json, devices/ciena/waveserver-ai.json, devices/adva/fsp-3000.json and devices/packetlight/pl-2000m.json, all of which pass scripts/audit-port-types.js. A real otn/wdm type is an engine change and would fix it for all of them.\n\nNot modelled: the OptiXtrans E9624X is a fourth, separate subrack - its own product page, its own SCC board (TNG1HCXP) and its own slot row in the vendor tables - not a variant of this one; see research/optixtrans-e9624x-fourth-subrack.md. No PoE and no copper access ports on this shelf at all.",
  "aliases": [
    "E9624",
    "OSN 9800 M24"
  ]
}
